{"h": {"measure": "R", "k": 100}, "sp": "nb", "rep": 101, "hyps": [{"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "When edge 5 is absent, the direct edge 0->3 carries more of the signal to the output, so upgrading it to a 3x3 conv helps more."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "avg_pool_3x3", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "With no direct input-to-output edge, activating edge 5 can restore a path to the output and give a large gain (experiments 55, 81)."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "direction": "lower", "why": "Deleting edge 3 costs more when it held a strong 3x3 conv than when it held a weak op such as pooling."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "avg_pool_3x3", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "direction": "lower", "why": "Replacing a 3x3 conv on the final edge with parameter-free pooling removes key capacity (experiments 18, 22)."}, {"edit_edge": 1, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 0, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "When node 1 is dead, edge 1 is the only way to compute at node 2, so a conv there matters more (experiment 26)."}, {"edit_edge": 4, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 4, "cond_op": "avg_pool_3x3", "direction": "higher", "why": "Replacing a pooling op with a conv adds learnable capacity, which gains more than replacing an existing conv (experiment 30)."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "direction": "lower", "why": "Swapping a direct 3x3 conv for an identity loses capacity, whereas replacing pooling or none with skip is often neutral or positive (experiments 47, 61 vs 54, 85)."}, {"edit_edge": 0, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 1, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "If edge 1 is already missing, removing edge 0 also cuts nodes 1 and 2 off from the input and collapses the cell's depth."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edi